---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import site from '../data/site.json';

const { baselineHeight, shrinkageRange } = site;
---
<Layout
  title="Legal & Disclaimers | 3D Print Miniature Scale Tool | STLACCESS"
  description="How I handle your data, scale conversion accuracy, and the legal fine print for STLACCESS — written in plain English, not lawyer-speak."
>
<div slot="page-header">
    <!-- Breadcrumbs -->
    <nav class="max-w-6xl mx-auto pt-12 pb-4 text-[10px] font-black uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-slate-600" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-[#ff6b35]">Home</a>
      <span class="mx-2 text-slate-800">/</span>
      <span class="text-slate-400" aria-current="page">Legal</span>
    </nav>

    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 mb-16">
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-6xl font-bold font-heading mb-6 tracking-tight">Legal <span class="text-[#ff6b35]">Stuff</span></h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-400 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">The simple, non-lawyer breakdown of how this site handles privacy, tech, and trademarks.</p>
    </div>
</div>

  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 pb-20 w-full">

    <section class="space-y-16" aria-labelledby="legal-content">
      <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none space-y-12 text-slate-300" id="legal-content">
        
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-12 not-prose">
          <!-- Accessibility Block -->
          <div class="bg-slate-900/30 p-8 rounded-3xl border border-white/5" aria-labelledby="accessibility-heading">
            <h2 id="accessibility-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-white font-heading mb-4">Accessibility</h2>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
              I built this site to be usable by everyone. It works cleanly with keyboard navigation and screen readers without getting in your way.
            </p>
            <ul class="text-xs space-y-2 text-slate-500">
              <li><i class="fa-solid fa-check text-[#ff6b35] mr-2"></i> High contrast layout</li>
              <li><i class="fa-solid fa-check text-[#ff6b35] mr-2"></i> Screen reader labels</li>
              <li><i class="fa-solid fa-check text-[#ff6b35] mr-2"></i> Simple, clean HTML structure</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <!-- Data Protection Block -->
          <div class="bg-slate-900/30 p-8 rounded-3xl border border-white/5" aria-labelledby="data-heading">
            <h2 id="data-heading" class="text-xl font-bold text-white font-heading mb-4">Your Data</h2>
            <p class="text-sm text-slate-400 leading-relaxed mb-6">
              There are no cookie banners, no personal data collection, and no invasive analytics here. The only analytics in use are basic, cookie-free pageview counts — no identifiable data is stored or shared.
            </p>
            <ul class="text-xs space-y-2 text-slate-500">
              <li><i class="fa-solid fa-check text-[#ff6b35] mr-2"></i> Cookie-free analytics only (Umami)</li>
              <li><i class="fa-solid fa-check text-[#ff6b35] mr-2"></i> Calculators run in your browser</li>
              <li><i class="fa-solid fa-check text-[#ff6b35] mr-2"></i> Open and honest setup</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Technical Stack -->
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-heading">The Tech Stack</h2>
          <p>
            I keep this engine incredibly light and fast so it loads instantly for everyone, everywhere — whether you are checking a scale conversion at your desk or pulling up a percentage at a tournament. Here is what runs behind the scenes:
          </p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-4">
            <li><strong>Plain JavaScript:</strong> No bloated frameworks. Things just work out of the box on pretty much any browser.</li>
            <li><strong>Tailwind CSS:</strong> Keeps the layout responsive so it looks solid on everything from phones to monitors.</li>
            <li><strong>Font Awesome:</strong> Simple, fast-loading visual icons.</li>
            <li><strong>Cloudflare:</strong> Makes sure the page stays online and loads quickly no matter where you are in the world.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <!-- Intellectual Property & Fair Use -->
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-heading">Trademarks & IP</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>STLACCESS</strong> is entirely independent. I am not affiliated with, backed by, or connected to Games Workshop Limited, Disney, Wizards of the Coast, or any other miniature brand out there.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-400">
            Names you recognize like Warhammer, Star Wars Legion, or Dungeons & Dragons belong completely to the companies that own them. When I mention them on this site, it is purely as a reference point to help fellow hobbyists calculate scale differences for their projects. It does not imply I own these names or have an official tie to them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Accuracy Disclaimer -->
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-heading">The Math Disclaimer</h2>
          <p>
            The tools here use scale math based on a {baselineHeight} human baseline. While the formulas are highly precise, treat the output as an excellent starting guide for your 3D printed miniatures. Real world factors like material shrinkage (typically {shrinkageRange} for PLA/PETG), specific slicer settings, nozzle size, or even how an individual sculptor stylizes their models can change how the final printed miniature looks on your tabletop. 
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Reporting Issues -->
        <div class="space-y-6">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white font-heading">See an Issue?</h2>
          <p>
            If something looks wrong, a conversion isn't hitting right for your prints, or you spot a problem with how the site handles layout accessibility, just open an issue on the GitHub repository. I built this for the 3D printing and tabletop gaming community and want to keep improving it.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>